All findings below are backed by `/home/namastex/prod/genie-desktop/src/main/services/ptyManager.ts` unless otherwise noted.

## 1. Live PTY session tracking

`src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:38`
```ts
const ptys = new Map<string, PtyRecord>();
```
A module-level `Map<string, PtyRecord>` keyed by session/PTY id. `PtyRecord` is defined at `ptyManager.ts:21-36`:

```ts
interface PtyRecord {
  proc: any; // IPty from node-pty
  cwd: string;
  isDirectSpawn: boolean;
  owner: WebContents | null;
  kind: PtyKind;            // 'agent' | 'shell' | 'tui' | 'service'
  taskId: string | null;
  featureId: string | null;
  mirror: TerminalMirror | null;
}
```
Records are inserted on every spawn path — `ptys.set(options.id, record)` at `ptyManager.ts:522` (`startDirectPty`), `ptyManager.ts:645` (`startPty`, shell fallback), and `ptyManager.ts:906` (`startCommandPty`, tui/service side-cars). Lookups use `ptys.get(id)` (e.g. `hasPty` at `ptyManager.ts:182-184`, `writePty` at `ptyManager.ts:686`), and removal happens via `ptys.delete(id)` in `onExit` handlers and in `teardownRecord` (`ptyManager.ts:768`).

## 2. Terminal resize

`src/main/services/ptyManager.ts:695-705`, function `resizePty`:
```ts
export function resizePty(id: string, cols: number, rows: number): void {
  const record = ptys.get(id);
  if (record) {
    record.mirror?.resize(cols, rows);
    try {
      record.proc.resize(cols, rows);
    } catch {
      // EBADF can happen during transitions
    }
  }
}
```
It calls `record.proc.resize(cols, rows)` — the node-pty `IPty.resize()` method — wrapped in try/catch since resize can race process teardown (comment notes `EBADF`). It also resizes the headless `TerminalMirror` in lockstep so a reattaching renderer gets a matching serialized state.

Wired from the renderer via IPC in `src/main/ipc/ptyIpc.ts:103-110`:
```ts
ipcMain.on('pty:resize', (_event, args: { id: string; cols: number; rows: number }) => {
  ...
  resizePty(args.id, args.cols, args.rows);
});
```

## 3. PTY shutdown/kill

Graceful kill with SIGTERM → grace window → SIGKILL, implemented in `gracefulKillProc` at `ptyManager.ts:707-763`:

```ts
// Grace window for a SIGTERM'd child to flush and exit before we force SIGKILL.
const GRACEFUL_KILL_TIMEOUT_MS = 3000;
```
(`ptyManager.ts:708`)

```ts
function gracefulKillProc(proc: KillableProc, timeoutMs = GRACEFUL_KILL_TIMEOUT_MS): Promise<void> {
  return new Promise<void>((resolve) => {
    ...
    timer = setTimeout(() => {
      try {
        proc.kill('SIGKILL');
      } catch { /* already dead */ }
      finish();
    }, timeoutMs);
    try {
      proc.kill('SIGTERM');
    } catch {
      finish();
    }
  });
}
```
(`ptyManager.ts:727-762`)

Timeout value: **3000 ms** (`GRACEFUL_KILL_TIMEOUT_MS`, `ptyManager.ts:708`).

The doc comment above it (`ptyManager.ts:710-719`) explains why SIGTERM (not node-pty's bare `kill()`, which sends SIGHUP) is used: SIGHUP isn't trapped by Claude Code, so its in-memory session tail was lost on refresh/quit; SIGTERM gives it a chance to flush first.

Callers:
- `killPtyInternal` (`ptyManager.ts:777-782`) — tears down the registry record via `teardownRecord`, then calls `gracefulKillProc(record.proc)`.
- `killPty(id)` (`ptyManager.ts:789-791`) — fire-and-forget wrapper.
- `killPtyAwait(id)` (`ptyManager.ts:799-801`) — awaits actual exit, used before respawning (`--resume`) to avoid a two-writer race on the session jsonl.
- `killAll()` (`ptyManager.ts:808-818`) — on app quit, persists all mirrors, kills every tracked PTY in parallel (`Promise.all`), so total wait is ~one grace window, not the sum.
- `killByOwner(owner)` (`ptyManager.ts:825-832`) — kills all PTYs owned by a closing `WebContents` (fire-and-forget).

`teardownRecord` (`ptyManager.ts:767-775`) removes the record from the `ptys` map and unregisters it from `activityMonitor`, `remoteControlService`, `contextUsageService`, and persists/disposes the mirror, before the actual process kill happens — making the map delete cause the original `onExit` handler to become a no-op (`if (ptys.get(id) !== record) return;` guards, e.g. `ptyManager.ts:543`, `ptyManager.ts:659`, `ptyManager.ts:919`).

## 4. Spawn environment / cwd / shell selection

Node-pty is lazy-loaded to avoid native-binding startup failures, `getPty()` at `ptyManager.ts:201-216`:
```ts
ptyModule = require('node-pty');
```

**Env for direct (agent/Claude) spawns** — built by `buildDirectEnv(isDark, cwd?)` (`ptyManager.ts:231-340`):
- Minimal env by default; full `process.env` inherited only if `syncShellEnv` is enabled (`ptyManager.ts:233-235`, toggled by `setSyncShellEnv`, `ptyManager.ts:190-192`).
- Always sets `TERM_PROGRAM: 'dash'`, `HOME`, merged `PATH` (prepends a Dash-managed `rtk` bin dir if present, `ptyManager.ts:238-242`), and `COLORFGBG` encoding dark/light theme (`ptyManager.ts:244-252`).
- Windows-specific: forwards `USERNAME` and a list of system vars (`APPDATA`, `LOCALAPPDATA`, `SystemRoot`/`SYSTEMROOT`, `COMSPEC`, etc.) (`ptyManager.ts:254-277`).
- Unix: sets `TERM=xterm-256color`, `COLORTERM=truecolor`, `USER` (`ptyManager.ts:278-282`).
- When not syncing the full shell env, passes through auth-related vars explicitly: `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, `GH_TOKEN`, `GITHUB_TOKEN`, proxy vars (`ptyManager.ts:284-303`).
- Merges user-configured env vars from settings (`claudeEnvVars`), blocked from overriding a `RESERVED_ENV_KEYS` set — `PATH`, `HOME`, `USER`, `TERM`, `COLORTERM`, `TERM_PROGRAM`, `COLORFGBG`, `DASH_HOOK_PORT` (`ptyManager.ts:127-138`, applied `ptyManager.ts:305-311`).
- Merges per-task/per-worktree port env vars via `WorkspacePortsRuntime.getEnvForWorktree(cwd)` so commands see the same host ports shown in the UI (`ptyManager.ts:313-324`).
- Sets `CLAUDE_CODE_NO_FLICKER=1` to disable Claude Code's own viewport scrolling since Dash renders its own terminal viewport (`ptyManager.ts:326-327`).
- Sets `DASH_HOOK_PORT` last (only if the hook server is bound / `port !== 0`) so ptyHookSettings' hook curl commands route back to the running app; deliberately absent for sessions launched outside Dash so the hook guard no-ops instead of erroring (`ptyManager.ts:329-337`).

**Agent binary/args/cwd resolution and actual spawn** happens in `startDirectPty` (`ptyManager.ts:405-558`): resolves an adapter (`agentRegistry.resolve`, `ptyManager.ts:450`), resolves the binary (`adapter.resolveBinary()`, `ptyManager.ts:458`), resolves resume-session id via `adapter.sessionDiscovery?.resolveResumeId(options.cwd)` (`ptyManager.ts:477`), builds the spawn plan via `adapter.buildSpawn(...)` (`ptyManager.ts:484-496`), writes Claude hook settings (`adapter.hooks?.writeSettings`, `ptyManager.ts:500`), then spawns:
```ts
const proc = pty.spawn(spawnPlan.command, spawnPlan.args, {
  name: 'xterm-256color',
  cols: options.cols,
  rows: options.rows,
  cwd: spawnPlan.cwd,
  env: spawnPlan.env,
});
```
(`ptyManager.ts:503-509`)

**Shell fallback spawn** (`startPty`, `ptyManager.ts:563-669`) has its own, simpler shell-selection logic:
```ts
const isWin = process.platform === 'win32';
const shell = isWin ? 'powershell.exe' : process.env.SHELL || '/bin/bash';
const args = isWin ? ['-NoLogo'] : ['-i'];
```
(`ptyManager.ts:582-588`) — deliberately interactive (`-i`) but not login, with a comment explaining login files add ~0.5s startup and PATH is already merged into `process.env` at app boot via a `fixPath()` step that runs `zsh -ilc` (`ptyManager.ts:586-588`).

Env for the shell path starts from a full `{ ...process.env }` copy with Electron artifacts stripped (`ELECTRON_RUN_AS_NODE`, `ELECTRON_NO_ATTACH_CONSOLE`, `ptyManager.ts:591-594`), then:
- On macOS, sets `TERM_PROGRAM = 'Apple_Terminal'` to enable zsh's OSC 7 cwd reporting (`ptyManager.ts:598-600`).
- If the shell is zsh, sets `ZDOTDIR = ensureShellConfig()` (`ptyManager.ts:603-605`) to inject a custom Dash prompt without clobbering the user's own `.zshrc`. `ensureShellConfig()` (in `src/main/services/ptyShellConfig.ts:60-86`) materializes `.zshenv`/`.zprofile`/`.zshrc`/`.zlogin`/`prompt.zsh` under `userData/shell`, chaining back to the user's real `$HOME` dotfiles then restoring `ZDOTDIR=$HOME` before sourcing them, and finally applying the Dash prompt (`ptyShellConfig.ts:14-51`).
- Merges the same per-worktree port env vars (`ptyManager.ts:613-615`).

Actual shell spawn call:
```ts
const proc = pty.spawn(shell, args, {
  name: 'xterm-256color',
  cols: options.cols,
  rows: options.rows,
  cwd: options.cwd,
  env: env as Record<string, string>,
});
```
(`ptyManager.ts:617-623`)

**Side-car/tui/service spawn** (`startCommandPty`, `ptyManager.ts:860-929`) uses caller-provided `command`/`args`/`cwd`, with env simply `{ ...process.env, ...(options.env ?? {}) }` (`ptyManager.ts:887-893`) — no special reserved-key filtering here, unlike the direct/agent path.

